{"step_index":96,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T15:45:34Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Interface Requirements**\n\nI'm currently dissecting the user's request to refine the StreamWave interface. The goal is clear: enhance it without a full overhaul or backend modifications. Right now, I'm carefully reviewing the nine specific requirements, starting with the playback labels, to better understand the scope and constraints of this task.\n\n\n**Refining Card Display**\n\nI've just focused on media cards, optimizing title display for longer names using two-line truncation and tooltips. Added clickable elements for the cover and title, and included hover/focus states to meet UI standards. The primary goal is to provide a good user experience on the new interface, which includes keeping equal card heights and avoiding needless visual embellishments.\n\n\n**Implementing Responsive Layout**\n\nI'm now focusing on responsiveness, planning a four-column layout for large desktops, scaling down to a single column on small mobile screens. I'll ensure horizontal scrolling is prevented across all devices. Accessibility considerations, such as visible focus indicators and accessible names, are next on my list, including a modal focus trap. Semantic HTML elements are also a priority.\n\n\n**Examining Index.html Structure**\n\nI'm now diving deep into `index.html`. It's a structured approach, scrutinizing the header with the StreamWave logo and essential links like Home, Catalogue, and a developer link. The hero will showcase *Cook Off* with a \"Watch Preview\" button. I'm also planning the main section's toolbar, state containers for loading and errors, the details modal's accessibility, and the audio player's controls. Finally, the footer needs non-commercial prototype details and metadata.\n\n\n**Styling Responsive Grid**\n\nI've just refined the CSS for the grid layout. I've defined a responsive grid that scales down from four colum